  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Using Fermat’s Little Theorem, show that the cube of any integer is of the form $7k$, $7k-1$ or $7k+1$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The possible cube residues modulo 7 are $0$, $-1$ and $1$.\n\nFor an integer not divisible by 7, Fermat’s theorem gives $(m^3-1)(m^3+1)\\equiv0\\pmod7$.",
      "worked": "Let $m\\in\\mathbb Z$. If 7 divides $m$, then 7 divides $m^3$, so $m^3=7k$ for some integer $k$. Otherwise Fermat’s Little Theorem gives $$\\begin{align*}m^6&\\equiv1\\pmod7\\\\m^6-1&\\equiv0\\pmod7\\\\(m^3-1)(m^3+1)&\\equiv0\\pmod7\\end{align*}$$ Since 7 is prime, it divides $m^3-1$ or $m^3+1$. Hence $m^3=7k+1$ or $m^3=7k-1$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. Together with the first case, this proves the result.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
